\title{Analyses for Paper II}
\begin{document}

% \section{Problem description}
% The purpose of this document is to argue for way to solve this problem: the data about forest coverage is only available for every fifth year, but the observations on living conditions happen every now and then, seldom on those particular years that we have forest coverage data for.

% We could interpolate one of them, or both. If we interpolate only forest coverage data we have a observations at years without any meaningful pattern, that might not be a problem in itself, or just a small problem. If we impute (arguably we can do better than just linear interpolation of local data in this case, since unlike the forest coverage data the living conditions data is always just sample data, the forest coverage data is the total population), living conditions ata we have data at every fifth year during the study period. The third option is to impute both types of data. This gives one measurement point every year during the study period.

% After some consideration, I'll decided to define the outcome as ``forest coverage change over the next 5 year'' to avoid having to interpolate forest change data. Living conditions data will be modelled (and hopefully more useful than the raw observed average).

\section{Matching point data from DHS with areas in the deforestation data}
Based on geocoded deforestation data for every fifth year from year 2000 up to 2020, and geocoded data on living conditions based on the DHS, construct a data set that includes an area average for household wealth index (IWI), GNI, and the change in deforestation for the forthcoming five years. The plan is as follows.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Import a dataset produced by the package globallivingconditions, this dataset contains individual records with GIS coordinates.
\item Discard unneeded columns from that dataset.
\item Import the administrative boundaries produced by the package globallivingconditions. This is a sub set of the boundaries in the published forest change dataset, and it is most efficient to work with this subset directly.
\item Use the unique GIS-coordinates from DHS to create a formal SpatialPoints object with the same CRS defintion as the set of boundaries
\item Use a ``find the area into which this point falls'' GIS-function to classify all points into areas.
\item Import the CSV version (non-geometric) version of the deforestation dataset.
\item Discard the rows in that dataset that represent areas which has no DHS data point or has no deforestation data (a few lakes)
\item Convert deforestation data from wide to long format
\item Make improved estimates of average IWI per area, based on years of education for a constant cohort (see below). Only try to fit a straight line for the change of IWI over time per area, we have too few points for curve-linear fits per area. We include years as dummies to capture global economic trends, so the total change is not necessarily linear.
\item Merge deforestation data for year $x$ with estimated livingconditions data for year $x-5$ by area names. This way we can use the living conditions estimates as an independent variable and deforestation data as dependent variable, since the (estimated) living conditions refer to the period \emph{before} the forest coverage data was recorded.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Forest change data}
The original source only provides data for every fifth year, starting at year 2000. 

